sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2023-goals-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In the evolved neural cellular automaton, the tissue temporarily moves away from its target pattern to remove misplaced cells and then returns (delayed gratification in the TAME framework), and since neither this nor long-term maintenance was selected for, such capabilities of multi-scale homeostatic systems can emerge as a 'free lunch' without direct selection.";
    dc:type "simulation-result";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Evolved tissues show delayed gratification as an unselected free lunch";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Pio-Lopez L, Bischof J, LaPalme JV, Levin M, “The scaling of goals from cellular to anatomical homeostasis: an evolutionary simulation, experiment and analysis”, Interface focus (2023), doi:10.1098/rsfs.2022.0072; section “Discussion”. Scope: In silico 2D neural cellular automata evolved for the French flag problem; multi-scale homeostatic systems"
}
